# BuddyNet VPS firewall (iptables), the iptables-restore equivalent of
# nftables.conf: default-drop input, with only SSH and the two BuddyNet UDP
# ports open. The HANDSHAKE port is rate-limited to blunt floods; the RELAY port
# deliberately is not, because it carries tunnel data (see the comment at that
# rule).
#
# Edit the two BuddyNet dports below to match your --listen settings, then:
#   sudo iptables-restore < deployments/iptables.rules
#   # persist with iptables-persistent / netfilter-persistent
#
# For IPv6, load it with ip6tables-restore and make BOTH edits first (see the
# IPv6 section at the bottom): (1) -p icmp -> -p icmpv6, and (2) the handshake
# rate limit's --hashlimit-srcmask 32 -> 64 (the shipped 32 groups by /32, an
# ISP-sized block; 64 is the per-subscriber granularity). WARNING: input policy
# is DROP; SSH (22) is allowed below so you are not locked out — change it if
# your SSH runs elsewhere.
*filter
:INPUT DROP [0:0]
:FORWARD DROP [0:0]
:OUTPUT ACCEPT [0:0]

-A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate INVALID -j DROP

# BuddyNet's UDP ports come BEFORE the generic ESTABLISHED,RELATED accept.
# Conntrack marks a UDP flow established as soon as it has seen traffic both ways,
# so once the server answers the first packet, every later packet of that 5-tuple
# matched the accept and the limit was never reached. The excess must also be
# dropped explicitly, or it falls through to the established rule.
# Two limits, and the ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING — per-source first, global second.
# A plain `limit` attaches its bucket to the rule rather than the sender, so one
# loud address can spend the shared budget; hashlimit gives each source its own.
# The global ceiling then only ever sees traffic that has already been tamed. The
# other way round it would be source-blind and drop a share of everybody's
# packets. Measured with lab/test-firewall-fairness.sh: a legitimate buddy keeps
# 15% of its throughput with the global limit alone, 77% with global first, and
# 100% in the order below. (hashlimit needs xt_hashlimit, part of the standard
# iptables modules.)
#
# ⚠ IPv6: change --hashlimit-srcmask to 64 before loading this with
#   ip6tables-restore (see the IPv6 notes at the bottom). --hashlimit-srcmask sets
#   the prefix length hashlimit groups sources by; 64 is one subscriber's
#   allocation and the intended granularity — it caps an attacker's whole /64 to
#   one bucket AND gives every other subscriber its own. The shipped 32 also caps
#   an attacker /64 to one bucket, but groups by /32 (an ISP-sized block), so
#   unrelated subscribers of one provider share a bucket and a buddy in the
#   attacker's /32 can be crowded out. (Omitting the mask is the opposite error:
#   hashlimit then keys the full /128, one bucket per address, letting an
#   attacker's /64 multiply its own throughput — though the global limit below
#   still bounds the box.) iptables (v4) REFUSES a mask above 32, so this file
#   cannot carry both values at once.
-A INPUT -p udp --dport 51820 -m hashlimit --hashlimit-above 50/second --hashlimit-burst 50 --hashlimit-mode srcip --hashlimit-srcmask 32 --hashlimit-name bn-hs --hashlimit-htable-expire 60000 -j DROP
-A INPUT -p udp --dport 51820 -m limit --limit 500/second --limit-burst 200 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p udp --dport 51820 -j DROP

# The relay port has NO packet-rate limit on purpose: it carries tunnel DATA, and
# a rate safe for a control plane would throttle the tunnel. Its ceilings live in
# the relay itself; bandwidth is a shaping/provider problem, not a packets-per-
# second rule. Restrict WHO may reach it (--allow-cidr, or remove this line and
# firewall the port to your buddies).
-A INPUT -p udp --dport 51821 -j ACCEPT

# Everything else that belongs to an existing conversation.
-A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT

# ICMP (use -p icmpv6 with ip6tables-restore).
-A INPUT -p icmp -j ACCEPT

# ── IPv6 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file loads with ip6tables-restore too, but TWO edits are required first,
# and the second one is load-bearing rather than cosmetic:
#
#   1. -p icmp            ->  -p icmpv6      (above)
#   2. --hashlimit-srcmask 32  ->  64        (the handshake rate limit)
#
# Why (2): --hashlimit-srcmask is the prefix length hashlimit groups source
# addresses by, and there are three cases. /64 is one subscriber's allocation —
# the intended per-subscriber bucket. The shipped /32 is coarser: it still caps
# an attacker's whole /64 to one bucket, but lumps every /64 of an ISP-sized /32
# together, so a buddy that happens to share the attacker's /32 (same large
# provider) is crowded out of the shared bucket — a topology-dependent case an
# attacker cannot force, but a real one. Leaving the mask off is the opposite
# error: hashlimit then keys the full /128 and an attacker's /64 gets one bucket
# per address, multiplying its own throughput (the global limit still bounds the
# box, so a buddy in a different /32 is not locked out — but the per-source
# fairness is defeated). nftables has no such caveat: deployments/nftables.conf
# masks IPv6 to /64 in the rule itself and needs no per-family edit.
#
# Verify either family with:
#   sudo ./lab/test-firewall-fairness.sh                      # nftables, IPv4
#   sudo ./lab/test-firewall-fairness.sh --iptables           # iptables, IPv4
#   sudo ./lab/test-firewall-fairness.sh --ipv6               # nftables, IPv6 /64
#   sudo ./lab/test-firewall-fairness.sh --iptables --ipv6    # iptables, IPv6 /64

# Management.
-A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT

COMMIT
